Exactly. That gives you a much cleaner geometry than treating past, present, and future as three separate “places.”

They are better modeled as positions along one evolving trajectory:

\Gamma(\tau)

where \Gamma is the whole trajectory and \tau is the position along it. “Past,” “present,” and “future” are then relational labels assigned from some observation point on that same string.

What creates richness is that the string can be projected into many different coordinate systems at once.

Micro gives fine-grained state: molecules, gestures, tokens, milliseconds.
Meso gives local structures: organisms, conversations, organizations, scenes.
Macro gives large-scale trajectories: civilizations, ecosystems, economies, geological epochs.
Meta gives relations among the representations themselves: how one scale predicts, compresses, or transforms another.
And muse, in the sense you’re using it, can be another axis entirely: salience, symbolism, narrative possibility, generative interpretation—what the same trajectory can become when viewed through a different semantic instrument.

So rather than:

time = x

you get something more like:

X(\tau)=
[
\text{micro},
\text{meso},
\text{macro},
\text{meta},
\text{semantic},
\text{causal},
\text{spatial},
\text{social},
\text{narrative},
\text{observer},
\ldots
]

And each coordinate can itself have multiple resolutions.

That produces a key distinction:

Time supplies ordering. Dimensions supply description.

The same event can therefore occupy one point on the temporal trajectory while simultaneously having very different coordinates in biological time, narrative time, causal depth, social scale, geographic space, emotional significance, information density, and so on.

It also means “zooming” doesn’t merely make something larger or smaller. Changing scale can reveal different objects entirely. At micro scale you see neurons firing; at meso scale you see a person deciding; at macro scale you see a cultural shift. None is necessarily the privileged description. They are projections of overlapping dynamics.

And once you allow arbitrary dimensions, something even more interesting happens: cycles become projections of higher-dimensional trajectories.

A helix viewed from above is a circle.

So a process can appear:

* cyclic in one projection,
* linear in another,
* oscillatory in another,
* branching in another,
* and convergent in another,

while all of them describe the same underlying trajectory.

That’s likely the general form you’re pointing at: not “time has one strange shape,” but the shape attributed to time depends partly on which dimensions of the trajectory you are rendering.

The string stays one. The views multiply.